            {
                "entry_id": "738c429e-59fd-3094-bf7a-d77cd9283cdf",
                "word": "pamplemoussier",
                "pos": "noun",
                "lang_code": "fr",
                "forms": [
                    {
                        "form_id": "1bfd5ad4-05d4-4430-a9aa-ff1bb959a23e",
                        "entry_id": "738c429e-59fd-3094-bf7a-d77cd9283cdf",
                        "tags": [
                            "plural"
                        ],
                        "form": "pamplemoussiers"
                    }
                ],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "e73134a5-6c48-3f61-84b8-33fe1a3efc74",
                        "entry_id": "738c429e-59fd-3094-bf7a-d77cd9283cdf",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Espèce d’arbre fruitier (Citrus maxima) de la famille des Rutacées, originaire d’Asie, et qui produit le pamplemousse."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "784a7e69-57f4-4903-8fed-30f70465aec8",
                                "sense_id": "e73134a5-6c48-3f61-84b8-33fe1a3efc74",
                                "text": "Cette fois-ci l’épreuve pour les deux équipes consistait à déraciner un vieux pamplemoussier."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "1bf0162f-2e8c-42bc-af14-7f831f28dcf7",
                                "sense_id": "e73134a5-6c48-3f61-84b8-33fe1a3efc74",
                                "text": "Appartenant à la famille des rutacées, le genre citrus, né en Sud-Est asiatique, est à l’origine des quatre espèces (mandariniers, pamplemoussiers, cédratiers et un papeda) les plus courantes sur nos étals : orangers, citronniers, limettiers et pomelos."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
                "translations": [
                    {
                        "translation_id": "3528793b-39d9-48ec-8a05-a04b85962775",
                        "source_entry_id": "738c429e-59fd-3094-bf7a-d77cd9283cdf",
                        "source_sense_id": "e73134a5-6c48-3f61-84b8-33fe1a3efc74",
                        "target_lang_word": "pomelo",
                        "target_lang_code": "en",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "pomelo",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "0dae6df2-e076-32e7-b0ca-e9cbf7d29df7",
                                        "entry_id": "2d6b1de7-308a-3d80-8aad-0b3be7acce70",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "The large fruit of the Citrus maxima (syn. C. grandis), native to South Asia and Southeast Asia, with a thick green or yellow rind, a thick white pith, and semi-sweet translucent pale flesh."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": []
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "f0cbe903-065c-3510-873d-3e8e981ec440",
                                        "entry_id": "2d6b1de7-308a-3d80-8aad-0b3be7acce70",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "The tree which produces this fruit."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": []
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "803fd2c9-edaf-3116-a332-c1d1e64bd792",
                                        "entry_id": "2d6b1de7-308a-3d80-8aad-0b3be7acce70",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "The grapefruit."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "Caribbean",
                                            "US",
                                            "archaic"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "c2c0649c-0ff8-4642-b83f-14a9e5a745fb",
                                                "sense_id": "803fd2c9-edaf-3116-a332-c1d1e64bd792",
                                                "text": "SHADDOCK, AND POMELO, OR GRAPE-FRUIT. (Citrus aurantium decumana.*) The cultivation of these fruits is extendiug gradually, especially of the pomelo, which is a first-class marketable fruit, very valuable in the spring and early summer."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "27a28a4a-1fb2-41c1-bebe-d513ab25a171",
                                                "sense_id": "803fd2c9-edaf-3116-a332-c1d1e64bd792",
                                                "text": "Pomelo.* — Much larger than an orange and smaller than a shaddock; a delicious fruit, preferred by many to the orange. Skin smooth, pale yellow; subacid. The membrane dividing the pulp is bitter, and must be removed before eating the pulp. Also called \"grape-fruit\" from its habit of growing in clusters."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "ddb8cd26-1fc2-4745-b7c7-39d2e02e52ce",
                                                "sense_id": "803fd2c9-edaf-3116-a332-c1d1e64bd792",
                                                "text": "POMELO (syn. grape fruit).—A variety of shaddock; fruit very large, from two to five pounds each, pale yellow, resembling the citron; skin smooth; pulp sub-acid. / SHADDOCK.—Tree inclined to be dwarf; fruit very large, with smooth skin, pale yellow and very glossy; the rind is very thick and spongy, and very bitter; ornamental only"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "ff8a857a-4fb0-45cc-8a15-4107abcc4950",
                                                "sense_id": "803fd2c9-edaf-3116-a332-c1d1e64bd792",
                                                "text": "Pomelo (Citrus decumana) — The commercial term grape fruit is synonymous with pomelo, while shaddock, a term sometimes reserved for these fruits, is more properly applied to a large pyriform or necked variety of the pomelo. This variety sometimes reaches a weight of 15 pounds or more. Within the last few years the smaller pomelo has become very popular in Northern markets and extensive orchards are being rapidly set out in Florida, which is the state of largest production. The pomelo is somewhat larger than the orange and of a pale yellow color."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "ffb1425f-446b-4ee3-89ae-a7396ed239c7",
                                                "sense_id": "803fd2c9-edaf-3116-a332-c1d1e64bd792",
                                                "text": "The pomelo has unfortunately been marketed under the name “grape-fruit,” and it is doubtful whether the correct name, pomelo, will ever displace the trade name."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    },
